window.PET_DATA["knowledge/grammar-06-comparatives.json"] = {"id":"grammar-06","type":"grammar","title":"Comparatives & Superlatives","objective":"Compare people and things accurately with -er/more, the -est/most, as...as, too and enough.","sections":[{"heading":"Comparatives","body":"Short adjectives: add `-er` (+ than): `faster than`, `happier than` (y → i), `bigger than` (double consonant).\nLong adjectives: `more + adjective + than`: `more expensive than`, `more interesting than`.\nIrregular: good → better, bad → worse, far → further/farther.","bodyZh":"短形容词加 -er（+ than）；长形容词用 more + 形容词 + than。不规则：good→better，bad→worse，far→further。","examples":[{"en":"Travelling by train is more relaxing than flying.","zh":"坐火车旅行比坐飞机更放松。"},{"en":"My new phone is much better than my old one.","zh":"我的新手机比旧的好得多。"}],"tip":"Add much / a lot / far before comparatives for emphasis: much better, far more exciting — examiners love it."},{"heading":"Superlatives","body":"Short adjectives: `the + -est`: `the fastest`, `the happiest`, `the biggest`.\nLong adjectives: `the most + adjective`: `the most beautiful`.\nIrregular: the best, the worst, the furthest.\nSuperlatives are often followed by `in/of`: `the tallest building in the city`, `the best day of my life`.","bodyZh":"短形容词用 the + -est；长形容词用 the most + 形容词。常与 in/of 连用。","examples":[{"en":"That was the most exciting match I have ever seen.","zh":"那是我看过的最精彩的比赛。"},{"en":"February is usually the coldest month here.","zh":"这里二月通常是最冷的月份。"}],"tip":"Ever after a superlative + present perfect is a classic high-level structure: the best film I've ever seen."},{"heading":"(not) as ... as","body":"Equality: `as + adjective + as`: `She is as tall as her brother.`\nDifference: `not as/so + adjective + as`: `The film wasn't as good as the book.`","bodyZh":"表示同等程度用 as + 形容词 + as；表示不如用 not as/so + 形容词 + as。","examples":[{"en":"Cycling to work isn't as fast as driving, but it's healthier.","zh":"骑车上班没有开车快，但更健康。"}],"tip":"not as...as is a polite, natural way to compare in Speaking Part 4."},{"heading":"too & enough","body":"`too + adjective` = more than wanted (negative): `The soup is too hot.`\n`adjective + enough` = sufficient: `He is old enough to travel alone.`\n`enough + noun`: `We have enough time.` `too many + countable noun`, `too much + uncountable noun`.","bodyZh":"too + 形容词 = 太……（负面）；形容词 + enough = 足够；enough + 名词；too many + 可数名词，too much + 不可数名词。","examples":[{"en":"There is too much traffic in the city centre.","zh":"市中心交通太拥堵了。"},{"en":"You don't need to worry — we have enough time.","zh":"你不用着急——我们有足够的时间。"}],"tip":"Position matters: enough goes after adjectives (big enough) but before nouns (enough money)."}],"commonErrors":[{"wrong":"more better","right":"better","why":"Better is already comparative — never combine more with -er forms."},{"wrong":"the most fast","right":"the fastest","why":"Short adjectives take -est, not most."},{"wrong":"enough big","right":"big enough","why":"enough follows adjectives."},{"wrong":"more happier","right":"happier","why":"Do not use more with -er forms."}],"summary":{"keyPoints":["Short: -er / the -est · Long: more / the most.","Irregular: good-better-best, bad-worse-worst.","as...as for equality; not as...as for difference.","too + adj · adj + enough · too many/much + noun."],"mustKnow":["much better than","the most ... I've ever","not as ... as","too much / enough"]},"linkedPractice":["reading"],"reviewItems":[]};
